# zengin4j is a byte-oriented library, so what git does to line endings on
# checkout is a correctness concern rather than a cosmetic one (R-T18).
#
# Without this file, GitHub's Windows runners check out with core.autocrlf=true,
# every LF in a committed fixture becomes CRLF, and a golden file that asserts on
# exact content fails on Windows and nowhere else — with a diff of invisible
# characters.

# Normalise text to LF in the repository and in every working tree, so a golden
# file compares equal on all three platforms.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The Gradle wrapper is generated. Leave it exactly as Gradle wrote it — the
# .bat needs its CRLFs for cmd.exe, and neither file is ours to renormalise.
/gradlew.bat -text
/gradlew    -text

# Test fixtures are bytes, not text, and nothing may rewrite them.
#
# Marked explicitly rather than left to git's heuristic, which looks for a NUL in
# the first 8000 bytes: a fixed-length record of ASCII digits and spaces contains
# none, so an all-ASCII corpus would be silently rewritten. The two committed
# today happen to carry high-bit bytes and are detected correctly — that is luck,
# not a property to rely on.
zengin4j-core/src/test/resources/conformance/input/** -text
zengin4j-core/src/test/resources/io/zengin4j/core/*Inputs/** -text

*.jar binary
